---
/**
 * The support page.
 *
 * New, and it exists because the app now links to it. `SupportCommands` from
 * `swift-support-kit` renders three Help items — the tracker, the feedback form
 * and `siteURL + supportPath` — and the third had nowhere to land: this was the
 * only site in the fleet with a /feedback/ but no /support/. The other eight
 * shipped both together.
 *
 * It routes rather than explains. Three channels, and the only thing a reader
 * has to get right is which one their problem belongs in — public tracker for
 * anything about the software, the form for anything that quotes their own mail
 * or contacts, mail for a licence. Everything past that is a link.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import {
  APP_NAME,
  DOCS,
  FEEDBACK_URL,
  ISSUES_URL,
  PRICING,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF,
} from "../config";
---

<Layout
  title={`Support — ${APP_NAME}`}
  description={`Get help with ${APP_NAME}: the public issue tracker, a private feedback form for reports that quote your own data, or email.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="wrap mx-auto max-w-[72ch] px-6 py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="mono text-accent">sec · support</div>
    <h1
      class="mt-3 font-display text-[clamp(34px,5vw,52px)] leading-[1.05] font-semibold tracking-[-0.038em]"
    >
      Support
    </h1>

    <div
      class="mt-12 text-[16px] leading-[1.7] text-fg-dim
             [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:mb-4 [&_h2]:font-display [&_h2]:text-[22px] [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-[-0.01em] [&_h2]:text-fg
             [&_p]:mt-4
             [&_ul]:mt-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:space-y-2 [&_ul]:pl-5 [&_ul]:marker:text-fg-muted
             [&_strong]:font-medium [&_strong]:text-fg
             [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[0.92em] [&_code]:text-fg
             [&_a:not([class])]:text-fg [&_a:not([class])]:underline [&_a:not([class])]:decoration-rule-2 [&_a:not([class])]:underline-offset-4"
    >
      <p>
        Three ways in, and which one to use comes down to a single question:
        <strong>does telling us what went wrong mean quoting your own data?</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>A bug, a question, an idea</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href={ISSUES_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">issue tracker</a> is the primary channel
        and the best place for anything that is not about your own mailbox — a tool that misbehaves
        on an empty folder, a client that will not wire up, a protocol question, a feature request.
        It is public, so the answer is there for whoever asks next.
      </p>

      <h2>Anything that quotes your own data</h2>
      <p>
        A real {APP_NAME} bug report often has to include a subject line, a chat, an account name or
        a contact. The tracker is public and permanent; the
        <a href={FEEDBACK_URL}>feedback form</a> is neither. No account is needed and the email
        address is optional — leave it blank and the report is anonymous.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>{APP_NAME} still makes no network connection of its own.</strong> Choosing Send Feedback
        in the app does not send anything: it hands your browser a URL and stops. Everything that URL
        carries — the app version, the macOS version, the Mac model and the language — is visible in
        the address bar, and on the page as four fields you can edit or clear before pressing send.
      </p>

      <h2>Licences and payment</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={SUPPORT_EMAIL_HREF}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a> for a key that will not activate, a
        lost key, or a refund inside the {PRICING.refundDays}-day window. A key is checked on your
        Mac and never sent anywhere, so a support reply is the only way we can look at one — quote
        the address you bought with rather than the key itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        What a key covers, and what keeps working without one, is set out in the
        <a href="/terms">terms</a> and in
        <a href={DOCS.licensing} rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/licensing.md</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Finding the Help menu</h2>
      <p>
        {APP_NAME} lives in the menu bar, so it only has an app menu while one of its windows is
        open. Choose <strong>Open Cupertino</strong> from the menu bar panel and Help ▸ Send
        Feedback is there. Opening this page or the form directly in a browser works exactly as
        well — the app only ever hands them over.
      </p>

      <h2>What to expect</h2>
      <p>
        Everything gets read, and most things get an answer within a few working days. A report that
        says which version, which macOS and which MCP client was connected is one that can usually be
        answered in the first reply rather than the third.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
